Andrew Duane USG <duane@magma.uucp> wrote in message
news:91o3p7$79e9$1@lead.zk3.dec.com...
> WHOA!
>
> The last reply, from Frank at mailint03.im.hou.compaq.com is quite
> detailed, but unfortunately mostly wrong.
>
> Whether a subsystem is static or dynamic (as reported by sysconfig -m)
> has nothing whatsoever to do with how you set variables in sysconfigtab
> and whether a new kernel needs to be built or not.  The sysconfigtab was
> developed exactly so that kernels did not need to be rebuilt just to
> change variables.
>
> First, the ONLY approved method to do so is with sysconfigdb. It is
> HIGHLY NOT RECOMMENDED to edit /etc/sysconfigtab manually.
>
> Next, whether a variable can be set at run time, or needs to have the
> system rebooted (NOT rebuilt, just rebooted) depends on the variable.
> To find out, use sysconfig -Q:
>
> # sysconfig -Q inet udp_ttl
> inet:
> udp_ttl -       type=INT op=CRQ min_val=0 max_val=255
> #
>
> This gives the type (INT = integer), the min and max values (0 and 255),
> and the supported operations. CRQ means CONFIGURABLE (at boot time),
> RECONFIGURABLE (at run time) and QUERYABLE.
>
> So for this variable, the system does not even need to be rebooted for
> the new value to take effect. As soon as you do:
>
> # sysconfig -r inet udp_ttl=<newvalue>
>
> the new value will be used.  If it were only CQ, the system would have
> to be rebooted to take effect.
>
>
> All of this is detailed in the sysconfig man page. If you have any
> more questions, feel free to email me.
>
