Christophe JEANSON wrote:
> 
> Hi !
> 
> I'm working in a small company, working in scientific computation. Our
> network (TCP/IP 10 base 100) is composed by an old
> DEC ALPHA SERVER 2000/233 (512 Mo of RAM) running OpenVMS 7 and 20 PCs
> with  NT and an X emulator software. Pre/post processing is made on the
> PC, calculation is made in batch mode on the server. At most, 5 batchs
> are ran simultaneously by the server. 

If you aren't planning on a major re-writing of your application, then why are
you posting this in Un*x newsgroups ?  No Un*x has a batch system similar (or as
flexible and powerful) as VMS !!!  The closest you would come to it is the 3rd
party (ie. extra $$$) app from Platform Computing called Load Sharing Facility. 
It works well, but it is no VMS batch system !!!


> The other users are responsible of
> very low activity (text editing, for instance). We don't perform
> parallel computation (No need).
> 
> At the beginning of 2001, we have planned to change our server. to my
> mind, the following speficiation are required :
> - at least 4 Go of RAM,
> - At least 2 processors (in order to endure high peaks of activity),
> - high performance 64 processors,
> - enough swap space (3 times the RAM space ?)
> 
> I propose the following configurations :
>     COMPAQ DS 20
>     HP J5600 or J6000
>     IBM 44P 270
>     SUNBLADE 1000 (2 procs at 700MHz)


First, when comparing different brands there is no benchmark like your
application(s).  Not having the manpower/time/money to "port" your application
to all of those systems, I strongly recommend you go to www.spec.org and look at
the results of the CPU2000 test (sorry IBM, HP, Sun you guys don't look so good
when it comes to crunching numbers !!)  You will have to figure out how to
correlate the CPU2000 test to your application.  Lastly don't forget price.  A
few years ago Sun cut the price of their Ultra60 almost in half when we wanted
to buy a large quantity of Ultra30 that were discontinued !!  (Stay away from
any thing using an UltraSPARC II processor; old technology, not cost effective).

Do you **REALLY** think your are ever going to grow to 64 CPUs ?  If so maybe
you want a "boat load" of smaller boxes (like DS10s).  As long as your I/O load
is not too high, NFS (or VMS Cluster), perhaps on a second network, would work
okay.  Remember, more boxes means more administration headaches (ask me how I
know).

> 
> MY QUESTIONS : (thank you for having read me until there).
> * Do you feel that this kind of conguration may fullfill our
> requirements ?

Yep

> * Are these workstation powerfull enough, or even designed to be used as
> a server in this environment ?

What really makes a machine a "server"?  I think most would agree, a balance
between CPU, memory and I/O capacity with probably an emphasis on I/O, both
speed and quantity.  How much disk are you looking at ?  RAID or JBOD ? 
UltraSCSI 2 and 10K RPM drive are probably all you need (you will pay premium
for Fiber Channel although some vendors will insist you need it)

> * Do you have, at work, a similar structure, and then what is your
> configuration ?

This sounds similar (but smaller) than what we have at my site.  We have 2
Compaq Alpha ES40s (first with 2 - 500 MHz CPU and 4 GB the second with 1 -500
Mhz CPU and 1 GB).  These replaced 2 Alpha 300M900 (275 Mhz 512 Mb each) and the
ES40s are **WICKED** Fast !!  A DS20 is literally 1/2 of a ES40 (2 CPU max
instead of 4).  If you can afford it, go with the ES40s even if you can only
afford 2 CPU's to begin with.  ES40s are (or soon will be) available in 3 speed
ranges, 500, 667 and 800 Mhz.  If you want to save money check into used 500 Mhz
systems (I have had good luck with a Compaq reseller called Great Lakes
Computer).  If you do go used, make certain you get in writing that the system
is capable of using the newer, faster CPUs (early system boards can not).  Also
save money buying used or 3rd party memory.

> * Do you have underwent the same kind of migration VMS -> UNIX ?Did you
> go into troubles ?

DON'T DO IT !!!!  You will regret it.  If it's not broken, don't fix it.  We did
a study that showed we could save $500K/year by going back to VMS and NT
(instead of VMS, Tru64, Solaris and NT).  This was politically the "wrong
answer" so it never happened.


Jack Patteeuw
(yes, I am biased, but hey, opinions are like a**holes, evryone has one)

I used to do VMS,
now I do Un*x,
it's a living !!


