>John - with respect, that doesn't make much sense, IMHO.

>I mean, the DEC guys designed and (initially) fabbed it. Why would
>their software guys hate it so?

I haven't had to work on its drivers, but one big shortcoming is that
it cat start receiving on an odd 16-bit boundary.  For TCP/IP, this is
a big deal (for ONC-RPC and NFS a bigger deal).  Ethernet headers are
14 bytes long, UDP, TCP, IP, RPC are all designed to work well with 32
bit alignment of the IP header.  Can't do that with Tulip, ya either
have to give up on 32 bit accesses and glue 16 bit accesses together or
copy the first X bytes of the message into another buffer.

>As for Intel - there were a bunch of people making cards with tulips
>on them - will they all dry up?

It all depends on whether they want to or can compete with Intel I
suppose.

>I've noticed that the DE6xx is an Intel Etherexpress - is Intel no longer
>fabbing tulips, thus Compaq's compelled to use Etherexpresses?

