If you ran /sbin/bcheckrc, that means you will
have mounted the /usr partition. It is likely
that the /usr partition is mounted on
partition 'g' of the boot disk (this is Tru64
default). Therefore, you may effectively be
trying to repartition the partition which
houses /usr, which is mounted, and hence 'In use'.

Hope this makes sense.

Paul.


