The gcc compilers produce incorrect debugging information - bad indexes
into tables.  They also produce inadequate debugging information to do a
really good job - especially if you are trying to debug optimized code.

On Tru64 systems, ladebug expects the compilers to produce the so-called
3rd-eye symbol table inside coff .o files (all the Compaq compilers do)
plus Compaq extensions that describe optimized code (inlining, split
lifetimes, etc.).  On Alpha Linux systems, Compaq uses Dwarf2 in elf .o
files.

Have you tried using the Compaq cc and cxx compilers and ladebug?  I
understand that there are advantages to using the same dialect/dev system
on several platforms, but my experience is that the Compaq compilers
usually produce better diagnostics, and almost always produce much better
code and better debugging information.

Apart from a few places where g++ would not accept valid C++, we had good
success using g++ to port Ladebug to Alpha Linux, although we have since
switched to using Compaq's cxx on that platform also.  The Compaq compilers
have command line switches that cause them to accept pretty close to the
gcc dialects on both platforms.

/Bevin Brett
Ladebug team
Core Tech Group, High Performance Technical Computing Group
Compaq

ps: On Tru64, use ladebug, not dbx.  dbx is being phased out.


