In article <90qbej$5ao$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, pedeja@my-deja.com writes:

>Hi All,
>Does this sound strange? I mounted a disk (RZDEC) which apparently has a
>corrupt or non-existant disk label. There is data there and probably
>there always has been.
>Can anyone explain how this is possible?
>Thanks,
>pe
>
>
The disk label and the file system on a disk aren't closely related; neither
requires the other be present.  When there isn't a label, the disk driver
provides a default partition table.  If you use the default or don't take
advantage of partitions at all, you don't need a disk label.  That said,
default partition tables may change.  If you're going to take advantage
of partitions it is wise label the disk and rely on the partitions there.
--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com



