I'm assuming you power on, and it jumps straight into the OS boot, and you 
don't know how to stop it.  

If that assumption is wrong, ignore this message.

Here are a few suggestions:
- Press the Halt button (if there is one) on the front of the machine.  
That's pretty sure to get you a SRM prompt.
- Press Ctrl-P, which *may* get you a SRM prompt.
- When you power on, keep tapping ctrl-C until a SRM prompt appears.
- If all else fails, disconnect power to the boot device.  That's SURE to 
get you the SRM or AlphaBIOS or whatever else instead of the OS.
- If you still can't see it, hook up a monitor, or connect to the serial 
port, whichever you haven't done already.

Here's some background info that you might be missing (If you're from the 
i386 background):
- SRM is a miniature OS in the firmware, and it is used in place of the 
BIOS in a PC.  It's far better and far more powerful.
- SRM doesn't automatically go from Floppy to CD to hard disk.  Instead, 
there's a variable, bootdef_dev, which indicates the default boot device.
- Without changing bootdef_dev, you can issue a one-time boot command to 
boot from any device of your choice, including CD.  (If you know the 
command)

If you are missing background in SRM, post another message, and I'll give 
you some starting pointers.


