Many of the Linux apps, provided in source form, can be built for Tru64 systems, but in
general they won't just run there.

The Tru64 threads are much better, so if you are looking at a pthreads app then that
will probably be sufficient to tip you in that direction.

If you decide to use Alpha Linux, there are a few basic things...

(a) get the Compaq C, C++, and F90 compilers, math, and debugger if you care about
performance.

(b) get the Compaq JumpStart CD (or download it and burn your own copy)

(c) be careful about making sure you have a graphics card and a ethernet card that are
linux-supported - they seem to give the most hassles

(d) RH 7.0 has just become available,  6.2 is probably the best shaken-down version
right now

