In article <907uvj$lc5$1@oslo-nntp.eunet.no>,
%username% <%username%@ssb.no> wrote:
> Hello !
> 
> We need a security administration system where we can administrate users
> such that users easily can have permit on files belonging to other
> deparments and such that permit expire on a preset date.

Access control lists (ACLs) allow the fine-grained control over permissions,
but there's no built-in support for ACL expiration.  It would not be hard
to write a wrapper around the ACL tools that used `at' jobs to change the
ACLs (removing permission for a particular user, perhaps) at a particular
time.  You would have to build part of the solution yourself, but it shouldn't
be difficult and would solve the problem you posed.

Be advised that most "archive" programs and even many backup programs don't
handle ACLs correctly.  tar, cpio, zip, et. al. *may* lose ACLs on files
contained within archives they create.

Tim
